olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Keys. Phone. Wallet. What day it is. The n=
ames of people to whom we=E2=80=99ve just been introduced. Where we parked =
the car. The shopping list we wrote to remind us what <del aria-label=3D"[s=
tart of struck out text] Tin [end of struck out text]" role=3D"text">Tin</d=
el> groceries we need. The dog we=E2=80=99re supposed to be walking. Dozens=
 of umbrellas. The name of the hotel we=E2=80=99re staying in. Assorted bir=
thdays. Why we walked into a room. The list of things your scatterbrained F=
ootball Daily forgets with alarming frequency is so long that we=E2=80=99re=
 genuinely struggling to remember most of the things that should feature on=
 it. While our absentmindedness tends not to upset anyone other than oursel=
ves or our nearest or dearest, we can=E2=80=99t even begin to imagine the b=
owel-loosening horror of completely forgetting something so simple and blin=
dingly obvious that it enrages the majority of the 74,161 football fans in =
attendance while bringing the already snakebelly low reputation of your ent=
ire profession into even more disrepute than it was less than 24 hours prev=
iously.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">And yet that appears to be exactly what ha=
ppened to Matt Donohue in Stockley Park on Sunday, when he made what can po=
litely be described as a complete dog=E2=80=99s breakfast of his review of =
the events leading up to the winning goal scored by Erling Haaland for 10-m=
an Manchester City <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c=
/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEBgtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPgHrlxF=
_IDDQ4KIitPNHLcuJDaNoe_oBI1FBPeEF5hLHarzMrENqf0lgFxafNEzUc5CvPcwXvBHOeZ02Mq=
9sGASxjgU_NiekkapzyDIiWKpZkfPAg2Tc-re-T1vnRfeqfZ_Jaao6JI7FHy5JSbGrBEAj9To6q=
-4SG8j_jbnzXohH5IMwyGi57d2wDCIbaX-N1Y3t0fUQBocQJz-Im_YSKX9L8TRfBiKeYBea7f7A=
RiJamos_lEOenawg_3Uoz51fUp/4u1/dT5HlavJRYGS671QNFvXUA/h29/h001.rk5Z4QHcnba8=
P9M8dWLdU2v9E5-K6L2DWPDVVHodpQY" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">in the derby at =
United</a>. His goal was immediately disallowed by the officials at Old Tra=
fford for an offside against the Norwegian that seemed fair enough until he=
 was subsequently vindicated by semi-automated offside technology. Our curt=
ain-twitcher-in-chief in the VAR bunker then had to decide whether there wa=
s any chance Enzo Fern=C3=A1ndez might have been interfering with play when=
 he =E2=80=93 from an obvious offside position =E2=80=93 flung himself full=
-length at the low cross Haaland subsequently converted, clearly distractin=
g the opposition goalkeeper and one of their defenders in the process.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In the lengthy deliberations that followed=
, our bungling video assistant referee=E2=80=99s repeated rocking and rolli=
ng of the footage suggested he was only trying to establish whether Fern=C3=
=A1ndez had made contact with the ball (he hadn=E2=80=99t). With all the di=
stracting babble going on in his headphones, we suspect that in the heat of=
 the moment, he actually forgot the Argentinian didn=E2=80=99t have to touc=
h the ball to have an impact on the situation sufficiently illegal to cance=
l out a goal that every single person watching =E2=80=93 except the only on=
e whose opinion mattered =E2=80=93 knew was offside.</p>
